.TH CSORT_GET_DEFAULT_COMPARISON_PROC 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
csort_get_default_comparison_proc \- get default comparison for a type
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/csort.h>

ccol_comparison_proc_t csort_get_default_comparison_proc(dummy_var);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro gives the default comparison function for a type. It works
through C11 _Generic, so the type of the dummy variable decides which
comparison function you get.

The supported types are all the standard C integral types (char,
signed char, short, int, long, long long and their unsigned variants),
bool, the floating-point types float, double and long double, and the
string types char* and const char* with any signed or unsigned character
type. A pointer that is itself const or volatile, such as a
static const char *const, counts as a string type too. The string
comparator puts a stored NULL before every string that is not NULL, and
a NULL is equal only to another NULL.

The comparator for bool puts false before true.

An enumerated type works as well. C makes every enumerated type
compatible with one of the standard integer types, and the macro gives
the comparison function of that compatible type.

For a type that it doesn't support the macro gives NULL, and that NULL
is part of its contract, because a caller with its own fallback depends
on it; cvec_find, for example, then compares the element byte-wise. A
caller that has no fallback must refuse the type at compile time rather
than at run time, which is what cvec_sort does.

A fixed-size char array (char[N]) is not a supported type, and it is a
different thing from a char* pointer variable, so the macro gives NULL
for it like for any other unsupported type. The bytes stored inline in
such an array are not a char* value, while
_csort_default_string_comparison_proc dereferences a pointer, so its
contract doesn't hold for an array. To sort a collection of fixed-size
string buffers, write your own comparator and pass it directly to
cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc() or to csort_sort().
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dummy_var  A variable of the type that you want to compare. The macro does
           not evaluate it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Gives a pointer to the right comparison function, or NULL when the
macro does not support the type.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro uses the variable only for its type and never evaluates it,
so dummy_var can be any expression of the type you want to compare. An
rvalue such as a cast or a literal is fine, and the expression does not
need an address.

For a real char* or const char* pointer variable the macro gives
_csort_default_string_comparison_proc, and that includes a pointer
variable that is itself const or volatile. For a fixed-size char array
(char[N]) it gives NULL, not _csort_default_string_comparison_proc. For
an enumerated type it gives the comparison function of the standard
integer type that is compatible with it.

An integral type compares three-way, as (a > b) - (a < b). For a
floating-point type (float, double or long double) a NaN operand is
greater than every value that is not NaN and equal only to another NaN,
so the result is always a real total order.

Every default comparator reads its two operands byte-wise, so the
address of an operand needs neither the alignment nor the effective type
of the element. That matters for a custom getter over a packed buffer.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
// Get the comparator for a string
char* dummy_str;
ccol_comparison_proc_t cmp = csort_get_default_comparison_proc(dummy_str);
// cmp now points to _csort_default_string_comparison_proc

// Get the comparator for a double
double dummy_double;
cmp = csort_get_default_comparison_proc(dummy_double);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR csort_sort (3),
.BR cvec_sort (3)
